  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 227 AM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nEarly this morning, a deep layer trough over the Midwest continues \nto trek eastward while an extensive ridge remains centered over the \nDesert Southwest. At the surface, the cold front that passed through \nthe area yesterday is moving south and east of the Ozarks. A tight \npressure gradient is present across eastern Kansas and western \nMissouri between a robust surface low over the Midwest and a surface \nridge sinking southward into the northern/central plains. As a \nresult, breezy northerly winds are expected today, mainly along and \neast of the Flint Hills. Wind gusts late this morning into early \nthis afternoon could exceed 35 mph at times across eastern Kansas. \nWinds should gradually decrease as the surface low departs eastward \nand the pressure gradient relaxes. These northerly winds will also \nserve to continue advecting milder air into the region. Temperatures \nwill only top out in the 80s this afternoon, and lows will dip into \nthe mid 60s tonight. While winds will be lighter and turn more \neasterly/southeasterly on Sunday, slightly below average \ntemperatures will linger with afternoon highs in the mid 80s and low \n90s, and overnight lows in the mid 60s.\n\n     MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY...\n\nMid range ensemble and deterministic model guidance continues to \nshow a rebound in temperatures, beginning on Monday, thanks in part \nto gradual height rises aloft from a weak upper ridge building \nacross the central plains. In general, afternoon highs will return \nto the mid to upper 90s throughout much of the week, with some \nlocations potentially reaching 100 at least one day of next week. \nDespite temperatures being slightly above average, dewpoints will \ngenerally stay at or below the mid 60s which should keep heat \nindices at or below 100. Meanwhile, the precipitation forecast next \nweek remains somewhat nebulous. The 00Z deterministic model suite \nseems to be in modest agreement that a weak frontal boundary/surface \ntrough will stall across Kansas and could potentially be the focus \nfor isolated low storm chances each evening/night late next week. \nThat being said, it remains unclear if sufficient \nmoisture/instability will be able to support storm development. \nTherefore, storm chances remain low for Wednesday and beyond.",
